-- title:
--   Følner sets, Liouville random walks, extensively amenable actions and piecewise-projective groups
-- statement:
--   Notions used in the literature on the amenability of Thompson's group $F$.
--
--   **Følner sets (Moore, p. 1).** For a group $G$ and finite sets $\Gamma, A \subseteq G$, `IsFolner Γ A ε` says $A$ is $\varepsilon$-Følner with respect to $\Gamma$: $\sum_{\gamma \in \Gamma} |\gamma A \mathbin{\triangle} A| < \varepsilon |A|$. `towerExp p n` is the tower function, $\exp_0(n) = n$ and $\exp_{p+1}(n) = 2^{\exp_p(n)}$ (p. 2).
--
--   **Random walks (Kaimanovich, pp. 8, 10).** A finitely supported function $\mu \colon G \to \mathbf{R}$ is a probability measure (`IsProbability μ`) when its values are non-negative and sum to $1$, and strictly non-degenerate (`IsStrictlyNondegenerate μ`) when the semigroup $\operatorname{sgr}\mu$ generated by its support is all of $G$. A function $f \colon G \to \mathbf{R}$ is $\mu$-harmonic on a set $S$ (`IsHarmonicOn μ S f`) when $f(g) = \sum_h \mu(h) f(gh)$ for every $g \in S$, and the random walk is Liouville (`IsLiouville μ`) when every function that is bounded and $\mu$-harmonic on $\operatorname{sgr}\mu$ is constant on $\operatorname{sgr}\mu$.
--
--   **Extensive amenability (Juschenko–Matte Bon–Monod–de la Salle, Definition 1.1).** For a group $G$ acting on $X$ and a set $Y \subseteq X$, `IsExtensivelyAmenableOn G X Y` says there is a $G$-invariant mean on the finite subsets of $X$ that is concentrated on the finite subsets of $Y$ and gives full weight, for each finite $E_0 \subseteq Y$, to the finite sets containing $E_0$. A mean is a finitely additive probability measure on all subsets, as in `Garrido.IsAmenable`.
--
--   **Thompson's group.** Throughout, $F$ is `CannonFloydParry.F`, the group of piecewise linear homeomorphisms of $[0,1]$ with dyadic breakpoints and slopes powers of $2$, from the published Cannon–Floyd–Parry bundle. The Cannon–Floyd–Parry missions prove that every tree diagram represents an element of it (§2) and that it is isomorphic to the groups of both standard presentations (Theorem 3.4, `CannonFloydParry.exists_mulEquiv_F1_F` and `CannonFloydParry.exists_mulEquiv_F2_F`), and that it is generated by two elements (Corollary 2.6, `CannonFloydParry.closure_mapA_mapB_eq_F`), so statements about finite generating sets and finitely supported measures on $F$ are not vacuous.
--
--   **Piecewise-projective groups (Monod 2013 and 2023).** For a subring $A$ of $\mathbf R$ and a set $E \subseteq \mathbf P^1$, `HB A E` is Monod's $H_B(A)$ (2023, §2, p. 3) for $E = B \cup \{\infty\}$: the subgroup generated by the elements of Monod's `Monod.Gpp` that are piecewise in $\mathrm{SL}_2(A)$ with breakpoints in $E$, intersected with the stabilizer of $\infty$. This is how the Monod bundle builds $H(A)$, and `HB A (Monod.P A)` is `Monod.H A` and `HB ⊥ Monod.ratPoints` is `Monod.HRat` by definition, so $H_{\mathbf Q}(\mathbf Z)$ is the published `Monod.HRat`. `ratSubring` is $\mathbf Q$ as a subring of $\mathbf R$, so `HB ratSubring Monod.ratPoints` is $H_{\mathbf Q}(\mathbf Q)$. `HC1RatRat` is $H^{C^1}_{\mathbf Q}(\mathbf Q)$ (Theorem 5.1, p. 14), generated by the elements of $H_{\mathbf Q}(\mathbf Q)$ that are $C^1$-diffeomorphisms of the line: $C^1$ with nowhere vanishing derivative.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Moore (p. 1) writes the Følner condition with right translates $A\gamma$, for Moore's product $f \cdot g = g \circ f$, "$f$ followed by $g$" (§2, p. 4); with the product of maps by composition used here the corresponding translate is $\gamma A$, and for any finite $\Gamma$ a set $A$ is Følner for right translates exactly when $A^{-1}$ is Følner for left ones. Moore defines Følner sets for a finitely generated group and a finite generating set $\Gamma$; `IsFolner` is stated for any group and any finite $\Gamma$, and the statements that use it add those hypotheses. Kaimanovich defines a random walk to be Liouville when its harmonic measure $\nu$ on the Poisson boundary is a point measure, and notes (p. 8) that by the Poisson formula this means "there are no non-constant bounded µ-harmonic functions on the semigroup sgr µ"; the bundle uses that form, which needs no Poisson boundary. A function on $\operatorname{sgr}\mu$ is represented by a function on $G$ whose values off $\operatorname{sgr}\mu$ play no role: harmonicity at $g \in \operatorname{sgr}\mu$ reads only values at $gh \in \operatorname{sgr}\mu$. Extensive amenability is stated relative to a subset $Y$ of the set acted on, so that the action of $F$ on the dyadic points of $(0,1)$ is the action on $[0,1]$ restricted to them; when $G$ maps $Y$ to itself this is exactly the extensive amenability of the action on $Y$, since the finite subsets of $Y$ are then a $G$-invariant family and the mean lives on them. Monod's $H_B(A)$ is "the subgroup where matrices are chosen in $\mathrm{SL}_2(A)$ and breakpoints in $B$"; the bundle takes the subgroup generated by those elements, which has the same elements once they are known to form a group. A homeomorphism of the line is a map of $\mathbf P^1$ fixing $\infty$, and its two unbounded pieces can carry different matrices, so $\infty$ is admitted as a breakpoint. Monod assumes that $B \cup \{\infty\}$ is invariant under $\mathrm{SL}_2(A)$, which makes the set of such elements a group; `HB` drops that hypothesis by taking the generated subgroup, and both instances used here ($A = \mathbf Z$ or $\mathbf Q$ with $B = \mathbf Q$) satisfy it. Likewise the $C^1$-diffeomorphisms in $H_{\mathbf Q}(\mathbf Q)$ already form a group, so `HC1RatRat` has exactly those elements.
-- source:
--   Moore, J. T., Fast growth in the Følner function for Thompson's group F, Groups Geom. Dyn. 7 (2013) 633–651, https://doi.org/10.4171/GGD/201 (arXiv:0905.1118v7, whose page numbers are used), pp. 1–2, Følner sets, with random walks from Kaimanovich, V. A., Thompson's group F is not Liouville, in Groups, Graphs and Random Walks, LMS Lecture Note Ser. 436 (2017) 300–342, https://doi.org/10.1017/9781316576571.013 (arXiv:1602.02971v3, whose page numbers are used), pp. 8, 10, and extensive amenability from Juschenko, K., Matte Bon, N., Monod, N., de la Salle, M., Extensive amenability and an application to interval exchanges, Ergodic Theory Dynam. Systems 38 (2018) 195–219, https://doi.org/10.1017/etds.2016.32, Definition 1.1, and the groups H_B(A) from Monod, N., Some comments on piecewise-projective groups of the line, Groups Geom. Dyn. 19 (2025) 459–476, https://doi.org/10.4171/ggd/883 (arXiv:2305.00796, whose page numbers are used), §2 (p. 3) and Theorem 5.1 (p. 14)

import Definitions.Def_Garrido_Amenability
import Definitions.Def_Monod_PiecewiseProjective
import Mathlib

/-!
# Notions around the amenability of Thompson's group F

Følner sets and the tower function (Moore, *Fast growth in the Følner function for Thompson's
group F*, arXiv:0905.1118, p. 1–2); harmonic functions and the Liouville property of a random walk
(Kaimanovich, *Thompson's group F is not Liouville*, arXiv:1602.02971, p. 8, 10); extensively
amenable actions (Juschenko–Matte Bon–Monod–de la Salle, *Extensive amenability and an
application to interval exchanges*, arXiv:1503.04977, Definition 1.1, p. 1); the groups `H_B(A)`
of piecewise-projective homeomorphisms of the line (Monod, *Some comments on piecewise-projective
groups of the line*, arXiv:2305.00796, §2, p. 3, and Theorem 5.1, p. 14).
-/

namespace ThompsonAmenability

open scoped symmDiff

section Folner

variable {G : Type*} [Group G]

open Classical in
/-- Moore p. 1: a finite set `A` is `ε`-Følner with respect to a finite set `Γ` when
`∑_{γ ∈ Γ} |γ A △ A| < ε |A|`. -/
def IsFolner (Γ A : Finset G) (ε : ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∑ γ ∈ Γ, ((A.image (γ * ·)) ∆ A).card : ℝ) < ε * A.card

end Folner

/-- Moore p. 2: the tower function, `towerExp 0 n = n` and `towerExp (p + 1) n = 2 ^ towerExp p n`. -/
def towerExp : ℕ → ℕ → ℕ
  | 0, n => n
  | p + 1, n => 2 ^ towerExp p n

section RandomWalk

variable {G : Type*} [Group G]

/-- A finitely supported probability measure on `G`: nonnegative weights summing to `1`. -/
def IsProbability (μ : G →₀ ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∀ g, 0 ≤ μ g) ∧ (μ.sum fun _ w => w) = 1

/-- Kaimanovich p. 10: `μ` is strictly non-degenerate when the semigroup `sgr μ` generated by its
support is `G`. -/
def IsStrictlyNondegenerate (μ : G →₀ ℝ) : Prop :=
  Subsemigroup.closure (μ.support : Set G) = ⊤

/-- Kaimanovich p. 8: `f` is `μ`-harmonic on `S` when `f g = ∑_h μ(h) f(g h)` for every `g ∈ S`. -/
def IsHarmonicOn (μ : G →₀ ℝ) (S : Set G) (f : G → ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ g ∈ S, f g = μ.sum fun h w => w * f (g * h)

/-- Kaimanovich p. 8: the random walk `(G, μ)` is Liouville when every bounded `μ`-harmonic
function on the semigroup `sgr μ` generated by the support of `μ` is constant there. -/
def IsLiouville (μ : G →₀ ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ f : G → ℝ, (∃ C, ∀ g ∈ Subsemigroup.closure (μ.support : Set G), |f g| ≤ C) →
    IsHarmonicOn μ (Subsemigroup.closure (μ.support : Set G)) f →
    ∀ g ∈ Subsemigroup.closure (μ.support : Set G), ∀ h ∈ Subsemigroup.closure (μ.support : Set G),
      f g = f h

end RandomWalk

section Extensive

variable (G X : Type*) [Group G] [MulAction G X]

/-- JMMS Definition 1.1, relative to a set `Y ⊆ X`: the action of `G` on `Y` is extensively
amenable when there is a `G`-invariant mean on the finite subsets of `Y` giving full weight to
the finite subsets that contain any given one. Means are finitely additive probability measures
on all subsets (of the set of finite subsets of `X`, concentrated on those inside `Y`). -/
def IsExtensivelyAmenableOn (Y : Set X) : Prop :=
  ∃ m : Set (Finset X) → ENNReal, Garrido.IsFinitelyAdditiveMeasure m ∧
    m {E | (E : Set X) ⊆ Y} = 1 ∧ m Set.univ = 1 ∧
    (∀ (g : G) (S : Set (Finset X)),
      m ((fun E => E.map (MulAction.toPerm g).toEmbedding) '' S) = m S) ∧
    ∀ E₀ : Finset X, (E₀ : Set X) ⊆ Y → m {E | E₀ ⊆ E} = 1

end Extensive

section PiecewiseProjective

/-- `ℚ` as a subring of `ℝ`. -/
noncomputable def ratSubring : Subring ℝ := (Rat.castHom ℝ).range

/-- Monod 2023, §2: `H_B(A)`, as the subgroup generated by the elements of `G` that are piecewise in
`SL₂(A)` with breakpoints in `E ⊆ P¹`, intersected with the stabilizer of `∞`. -/
def HB (A : Subring ℝ) (E : Set (OnePoint ℝ)) : Subgroup (OnePoint ℝ ≃ₜ OnePoint ℝ) :=
  Subgroup.closure {f | f ∈ Monod.Gpp ∧ Monod.IsPiecewiseProjOn A E f} ⊓ Monod.fixInf

/-- Monod 2023, Theorem 5.1: `H^{C¹}_ℚ(ℚ)`, the subgroup of `H_ℚ(ℚ)` of `C¹`-diffeomorphisms of
the line, here generated by those elements. -/
def HC1RatRat : Subgroup (OnePoint ℝ ≃ₜ OnePoint ℝ) :=
  Subgroup.closure {f | f ∈ HB ratSubring Monod.ratPoints ∧
    ∃ u : ℝ → ℝ, ContDiff ℝ 1 u ∧ (∀ x, deriv u x ≠ 0) ∧ ∀ x : ℝ, f (x : OnePoint ℝ) = u x}

end PiecewiseProjective

end ThompsonAmenability


